# --- Python ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# --- Virtual environments ---
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- Notebooks ---
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- Test cache ---
.pytest_cache/
.coverage

# --- Environment / secrets ---
.env
*.local

# --- IDE / OS ---
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# --- Data (keep the folders, ignore the contents) -----------------------------
# Generated books and intermediate tables are reproducible from a seed, so they
# are not version-controlled. data/sample/ IS committed: a fresh clone must be
# able to run something.
#
# NOTE the shape of these rules. An earlier project in this portfolio wrote
# `data/*` here, which silently swallowed its own committed sample and made a
# fresh clone crash. Ignore the two generated directories by name, nothing more.
data/raw/*
data/processed/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep
!data/processed/.gitkeep

# --- DuckDB ---
# Ignore the WORKING warehouse by path, not every .duckdb file by extension.
# A blanket `*.duckdb` rule would also exclude data/sample/sample_book.duckdb,
# which is committed on purpose so a fresh clone can run immediately. That is
# precisely the mistake a sister project made with `data/*`, and it was found
# only when a clean clone crashed.
data/processed/*.duckdb
data/processed/*.duckdb.wal
*.duckdb.wal

# --- SAS ---
*.sas7bdat
*.log
*.lst

# The CSV bridge to SAS. Both directions are generated, not authored:
# sas/data/ is written by scripts/export_for_sas.py and is ~21 MB, and
# sas/results/ holds whatever the SAS run produced and is downloaded back.
#
# sas/*.sas IS committed, including the generated 00_config.sas, because the
# SAS side has to be readable without running anything. Note the shape of
# these rules: they name the two data folders, not `sas/*`, which would
# swallow the programs themselves. That is the mistake made once already with
# `data/*` on the scorecard project, where a clean clone crashed.
sas/data/*.csv
sas/results/*.csv
!sas/results/.gitkeep
